{# Home-page hero (architecture.md Phase 14, restyled to DESIGN.md § "Homepage" / design
   reference 5a): full-bleed cover photo behind the transparent site header (see .has-hero in
   header.css/base.njk). Everything else — headline block (orange 56×3 rule → H1 → tagline →
   intro), the calculator card, and the "Scroll down" cue — stacks inside .hero__cover and
   bottom-aligns against the photo's lower edge (the reference puts the calculator and the cue
   both inside the cover, the cue last). Desktop: the photo fills the viewport.

   src/assets/img/hero/home/ holds the chosen photo plus a couple of standby favorites (see
   _data/heroCredits.json for source/license per file — all Type1 KOGL, attribution required).
   TO SWAP: change heroPhoto below to another filename from that folder (dev server hot-reloads).
   Leave it "" to see the gradient placeholder. #}
{% set heroPhoto = "mungyeong-saejae-open-set.jpg" %}
{% set heroCredit = heroCredits.photos[heroPhoto] %}

<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-heading">
  <div class="hero__cover">
    <div
      class="hero__media{% if not heroPhoto %} hero__media--placeholder{% endif %}"
      data-hero-media
      aria-hidden="true"
      {% if heroPhoto %}style="background-image: url('/assets/img/hero/home/{{ heroPhoto }}')"{% endif %}
    ></div>
    <div class="hero__scrim{% if heroCredit.needsDarkerScrim %} hero__scrim--dark{% endif %}" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    {% if heroCredit %}
      {# CLAUDE.md rule 5: each KTO-derived block (region.njk, visa-info.njk) carries its own
         provenance — same pattern here, phrased the same way as those. #}
      <p class="hero__credit">
        Photo source: © Korea Tourism Organization (한국관광공사)
      </p>
    {% endif %}
    <div class="hero__content">
      <span class="hero__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <h1 id="hero-heading" class="hero__heading">{{ site.name }}</h1>
      <p class="hero__tagline">{{ site.tagline }}</p>
      <p class="hero__intro">
        Korea's F-1-D Digital Nomad Visa lets you live and work remotely from Korea for up to
        three years — but the income bar is lower, and life is better, outside the Seoul capital
        area.<br>
        Find out which regions you qualify for, then see what a month-long stay actually looks
        like there.
      </p>
    </div>

    {# Calculator (Phase 16): real <form>, calculator.js calls eligibility.js and renders the
     caption itself into #eligibility-result below. JS-only per CLAUDE.md rule 6 — the
     <noscript> fallback sends no-JS visitors to the static thresholds table instead.
     Markup order here is free to change (calculator.js keys off data-* / element names, not
     DOM order); the comparison logic and its messages are frozen. #}
  <div class="hero__calculator">
    <div id="calculator" class="calculator-mount" data-calculator-mount>
      <noscript>
        <p class="calculator-mount__noscript">
          The eligibility calculator needs JavaScript. See the
          <a href="/visa-info/">visa income thresholds</a> directly instead.
        </p>
      </noscript>
      <form class="calculator-form" data-calculator-form novalidate hidden>
        {# Card title + subtitle (design reference 5a). Inline UI microcopy, like the other hero
           strings — no _data home yet; see HANDOFF's "inline microcopy" note. #}
        <p class="calculator-form__title">Check which regions accept your income</p>
        <p class="calculator-form__note">The F-1-D visa sets a different income floor inside and
          outside the capital area. Nothing is stored.</p>
        <div class="calculator-form__row">
          <div class="calculator-form__field">
            <label for="calc-salary">Annual salary, gross (KRW)</label>
            <input
              type="number"
              id="calc-salary"
              name="salaryKrw"
              inputmode="numeric"
              min="0"
              step="1000000"
              required
            >
          </div>
          <div class="calculator-form__field calculator-form__field--age">
            <label for="calc-age">Age</label>
            <input type="number" id="calc-age" name="age" inputmode="numeric" min="18" max="120" required>
          </div>
          <button type="submit" class="calculator-form__submit">Check eligibility</button>
        </div>
        <div class="calculator-form__controls">
          <div class="calculator-form__field calculator-form__field--checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" id="calc-has-family" name="hasFamily">
            <label for="calc-has-family">Bringing spouse or children</label>
          </div>
          {# For a visitor who doesn't know their numbers yet (or wants to browse before
             committing to a check) — restores every region to open/active without needing a
             second, throwaway salary/age guess just to unlock the map. calculator.js wires this
             to the same applyResult() path a real "all eligible" tier would take. #}
          <button type="button" class="calculator-form__reset" data-calculator-reset>Reset the map</button>
        </div>
      </form>

      {# Result-caption mount point — calculator.js (Phase 16) owns all caption rendering here;
         korea-map.js (Phase 17) only applies map state, it doesn't touch this element. Sits
         inside the card but outside the <form> so form.reset() and form.elements never see it. #}
      <p
        id="eligibility-result"
        class="eligibility-result"
        data-eligibility-result
        aria-live="polite"
        hidden
      ></p>
    </div>
  </div>

    {# Static nudge toward the map below — a real anchor link (CLAUDE.md rule 6: works with JS
       off, no scroll-triggered JS needed) rather than tied to a calculator result, so it's
       useful to a visitor who skips the calculator entirely too. Sits at the foot of the cover,
       over the photo (design reference 5a). #}
    <a class="hero__scroll-cue" href="#map">Scroll down to explore the regions ↓</a>
  </div>

  {# Reference data for calculator.js — assembled at build time (see
     _data/eligibilityReferenceData.js), embedded here so the calculator needs no network
     request and no server. Exactly the referenceData shape eligibility.js expects. #}
  <script type="application/json" id="eligibility-reference-data">{{ eligibilityReferenceData | dump | safe }}</script>
</section>
